cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this software, please cite it using the metadata below."
title: "XRD Rietveld Plot Generator: a validated Jupyter notebook for publication-quality Rietveld plots from GSAS-II CSV exports"
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: Galeazzi
    given-names: Cristian
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0009-0006-7150-2659"
    affiliation: "Center for Materials Research (ZfM/LaMa), Justus Liebig University Giessen, Germany"
abstract: >-
  Turns the CSV a Rietveld refinement saves from its pattern plot into a
  publication-ready two-panel figure. The upper panel holds the observed,
  calculated and background patterns with one row of reflection markers per
  phase, the lower panel holds the weighted or unweighted residuals, and
  both are saved as a 600 dpi raster and a vector PDF. Phases are detected
  from the export itself rather than from a list of names, so any refined
  system is plotted without configuration. Every file in a folder is
  processed in one run, unreadable files are isolated without stopping the
  batch, and sample names, phase fractions and per-sample plot windows are
  read from a separate metadata file kept out of the repository. An
  optional panel replots one file with different limits before the batch is
  repeated. A second figure stacks a whole sample series on one set of axes,
  each pattern rescaled to the same height above the one below, for the
  question a pile of separate figures answers badly: which reflection
  appears, moves or goes away across the series. Which samples it holds and
  in what order is read from the same private metadata file, so no sample is
  named in the code. Parsing is bit-exact for both decimal marks and is
  asserted against synthetic data with zero tolerance. The routines live in
  plain Python modules, so the notebook, the test suite and any script share
  one implementation.
repository-code: "https://github.com/cristian-galeazzi/XRD-Plotter"
